 transfers: [#page, #service, #governance]
}

^["recursive_improvement"]{
 loop: [
  "? reread surface status and adjacent execution lanes before widening governance",
  "< ship one clearer gate or routing rule that changes a real launch decision",
  "$ record which public-surface claim became easier to trust or discuss",
  "^ feed the stable governance language into release, DX, and curriculum plans",
  "% keep governance alive by tying every taxonomy change to a real readiness judgment",
 ]
 gates: [#launch_discipline, #surface_truth, #non_ornamental]
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-03-26 20:22] observe — review found count drift across .spw/surfaces index and domains registry plus missing routing/governance semantics for many public surfaces.
 >>[2026-03-26 20:24] decide — patch set will preserve the in-flight status cleanup in .spw/surfaces/domains.spw and extend it with launch governance rather than replacing it.
 >>[2026-03-26 20:26] decide — update only the plans most responsible for release gating, QA telemetry, literature traceability, linguistic stability, and contributor formation.
 >>[2026-03-26 20:34] revise — added launch governance, hub segmentation, and explicit backlog/blocked routing semantics to the surface registry.
 >>[2026-03-26 20:36] revise — updated release, DX, telemetry, axis traceability, phase vocabulary, and web-platform curriculum plans to point at ecosystem governance rather than isolated internal craft.
 >>[2026-03-26 20:37] decide — docs/toc should expose the .spw surfaces registry directly so portfolio governance is discoverable from the main documentation map.
 >>[2026-03-27 00:30] observe — site codebases (factshift.com, boon.land, lore.land) need a governed installation path: which .spw surfaces ship as dependencies, which are bootstrapped by spw init, and how governance metadata flows from this registry into each site's local .spw/mount.spw.
 >>[2026-03-27 00:31] decide — the launch ladder should include an explicit "installable" gate between internal-tool and public-beta: a surface is installable when its .spw spec, LSP support, and CLI affordances are coherent enough for a site codebase to depend on them without pulling the full workbench.
 >>[2026-03-27 09:12] revise — integration model changed to git submodule at .spw/_workbench. "Installable" now means: the surface is engageable through the submodule mount protocol, not publishable as an npm package. The launch ladder gate should test whether a site codebase can mount the surface via .spw/mount.spw and have it resolve through _workbench.
 >>[2026-03-27 13:16] decide — governance is the canopy, not the bottleneck. Site-install, DX, CLI, editor, and release plans can run in parallel as long as each one names the installable gate and routes readiness claims back through this registry.
 >>[2026-03-27 15:15] decide — governance also needs plan-cluster semantics so execution truth lanes, curricula, discussion/exhibit work, and speculative futures stop competing for one false sense of urgency.
 >>[2026-03-27 15:16] revise — contributor-formation and research lanes stay valued inside governance, but they should feed heuristics and experiments into public surfaces rather than quietly masquerading as release blockers.
}

